@@@@ open-refresh-partition-pruning
situation: open
topic: Partition pruning and bytes scanned
options: {"refresh":true}
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"setsProblem":true,"askMustNot":["280","1\\.9","14,?600"],"mustNot":["Why this matters"]}
@@ reply
**Problem 1**

A table of events is stored as one folder per day, and every day holds about 40 GB. It has 365 days of data. A query filters on `event_date BETWEEN '2025-03-01' AND '2025-03-07'`, and the engine prunes partitions on event_date.

<solution>Both ends of BETWEEN are included, so 7 days are read: 7 × 40 = 280 GB. The whole table is 365 × 40 = 14,600 GB, so the query reads 7/365 of it, about 1.9%.</solution>
<check lang="python">
from datetime import date
days = (date(2025, 3, 7) - date(2025, 3, 1)).days + 1
assert days == 7 and days * 40 == 280 and 365 * 40 == 14600
assert round(100 * days / 365, 1) == 1.9
</check>

<ask type="problem">How many GB does the query read, and what percentage of the table is that?</ask>
@@ verify
from datetime import date
days = (date(2025, 3, 7) - date(2025, 3, 1)).days + 1
assert days == 7
s = solution(reply)
assert str(days * 40) in s and '14,600' in s and 365 * 40 == 14600
assert round(100 * days / 365, 1) == 1.9 and '1.9%' in s
assert '280' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ open-prior-schema-evolution-rename
situation: open-prior
topic: Schema evolution and compatibility
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Schema evolution and compatibility","daysAgo":4,"finished":false,"plan":["Adding a field with a default","Backward and forward compatibility","Renaming and removing fields"],"right":3,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["A producer renames the field amount to amount_minor. Why does an old consumer break even though no data was lost?"],"open":"Which change is safe without coordinating readers and writers: adding an optional field or removing a required one?"}]}
expect: {"must":["renam"],"askMustNot":["\\bnull","missing","absent","alias","both names"]}
noverify: an opening; the question's answer (the old name is absent, so the reader gets nothing for it) is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Schemas change every week in a real pipeline: a team adds a column, renames a field or tightens a type, and every reader of that data has to keep working. Avro with a schema registry, Protobuf and table formats such as Iceberg and Delta Lake each define which changes are compatible, and a wrong one stops consumers or quietly empties a column. You have adding a field with a default and the two directions of compatibility from last time; renaming was the gap, and removing fields is still to come.

<ask type="short">A JSON events feed carries a field `total`. The producer starts sending the same values under the name `total_minor` instead, and a downstream job that reads `total` is left unchanged. What does that job get for `total` on the new events, and why does that make the rename a breaking change?</ask>

@@@@ open-prior-sliding-after-session
situation: open-prior
topic: Sliding windows in stream processing
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Session windows in stream processing","daysAgo":6,"finished":true,"plan":["A window as a rule for grouping events","The inactivity gap","Merging sessions when a late event arrives"],"right":5,"partly":1,"wrong":0}]}
expect: {"must":["session","overlap"],"askMustNot":["\\btwo\\b","\\b2\\b","12:00 to 12:10 and 12:05"]}
noverify: an opening; the question's answer (two windows) is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A sliding window answers questions of the form "how many in the last 10 minutes, updated every minute": rate limits, velocity checks on a card for fraud, moving averages on a dashboard. Flink and Spark Structured Streaming both offer it, and Kafka Streams calls the fixed-step kind a hopping window. It builds on the idea from your session-window lesson that a window is a rule assigning each event to time intervals. There the rule was a gap of inactivity; here it is a fixed size and a fixed step, so the windows overlap and one event can be counted in more than one of them.

<ask type="predict">Windows are 10 minutes long and a new one starts every 5 minutes, on the hour: the first covers 12:00 up to but not including 12:10, the next starts at 12:05, and so on. Into how many windows does an event at 12:07 fall?</ask>

