@@@@ correct-skew-probe-to-plan
situation: after-correct
topic: Data skew and salting in distributed joins
voice: terse
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"need":["plan","note"],"must":["30 million","\\b60\\b"],"askMustNot":["15 million","15,000,000"]}
@@ tutor
**Why this matters**

A distributed join sends every row to a partition chosen by a hash of its join key, so all rows with the same key land on one task. When one key holds a large share of the rows, that task does most of the work while the others finish early, and the job is as slow as its largest partition. It is the usual reason a Spark or Trino job sits at 199 of 200 tasks, and the same effect unbalances Kafka partitions and sharded databases.

<ask type="predict">A join on customer_id is hash-partitioned into 200 partitions. One customer accounts for 30% of the 1 billion order rows. Roughly how many rows does the largest partition hold, and how does that compare with an even split?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
300M+ in one partition, all of that customer's rows hash to the same place. even split would be 5M each. so ~60x bigger
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"all of that customer's rows hash to the same place" is the whole cause: one partition gets the 300 million rows plus its ordinary share, about 60 times the 5 million of an even split.</grade>

<plan>
- Why more partitions do not help
- Salting the hot key
- What salting costs on the other side of the join
</plan>

Raising the partition count does not split a hot key: a hash sends equal keys to the same partition however many partitions there are. Salting changes the key itself. Append a random number from 0 to $s - 1$ to the key on the skewed side, and the hot key becomes $s$ different keys that can be spread over up to $s$ partitions. With $s = 10$, the 300 million rows become ten keys of about 30 million rows each.

<note>Skew: equal join keys always hash to the same partition, so one hot key makes one oversized task. Salting: join on (key, salt), with salt random in 0 to s − 1 on the skewed side, which cuts the hot key's largest group to about 1/s of its rows.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

One key has 120 million rows. It is salted with $s = 8$, and the 8 salted keys happen to land in 8 different partitions.

<solution>120 million / 8 = 15 million of the hot key's rows per salted key, so about 15 million in each of those partitions.</solution>
<check lang="python">
assert 120_000_000 / 8 == 15_000_000
</check>

<ask type="problem">About how many of that key's rows does each of those partitions receive?</ask>
@@ verify
rows, parts, share = 1_000_000_000, 200, 0.30
hot = rows * share
even = rows / parts
assert hot == 300_000_000 and even == 5_000_000
largest = hot + (rows - hot) / parts          # the hot key plus an ordinary share of the rest
assert 60 <= largest / even < 61               # "about 60 times", and the learner's "~60x"
assert hot / 10 == 30_000_000                  # s = 10 in the worked example
assert 120_000_000 / 8 == 15_000_000 and '15 million' in solution(reply)
assert '15 million' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ correct-bytes-scanned-harder
situation: after-correct
topic: The cost of a full scan: bytes scanned
options: {"difficulty":"hard"}
voice: plain
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["1\\.2 GB","0\\.006","\\b40 times"],"mustNot":["should have"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 1 · hard**

A columnar table `payments` has 2 billion rows and 50 columns. Assume every column takes the same space and the table is 800 GB compressed. It is not partitioned. A query runs `SELECT user_id, amount FROM payments WHERE country = 'NG'` on an engine that charges 5 dollars per TB scanned (1 TB = 1,000 GB).

<solution>Three columns are read: user_id, amount and country (the filter column must be read as well as the two returned). Each column is 800 / 50 = 16 GB, so the scan is 48 GB = 0.048 TB, costing 0.048 × 5 = 0.24 dollars.</solution>
<check lang="python">
per_col = 800 / 50
scan = 3 * per_col
assert per_col == 16 and scan == 48 and abs(scan / 1000 * 5 - 0.24) < 1e-12
</check>

<ask type="problem">How many GB does the query scan, and what does it cost? Say which columns you counted.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
Three columns, not two: user_id and amount are returned and country has to be read for the WHERE. 800/50 = 16 GB per column, so 48 GB = 0.048 TB, about $0.24.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"country has to be read for the WHERE" is the step most answers miss: a filter column is scanned even though it is not returned, so 3 × 16 = 48 GB and 0.24 dollars.</grade>

**Problem 2 · harder**

The table is rebuilt partitioned by day on `pay_date`: 400 days of data, every day the same size, still 800 GB in total and 50 equal columns. Assume pay_date is held in the partition's path, so reading it costs no bytes, and the engine prunes partitions on it. The query becomes `SELECT user_id, amount FROM payments WHERE country = 'NG' AND pay_date >= '2025-06-01' AND pay_date < '2025-06-11'`.

<solution>The range covers 10 days (1 to 10 June), so 10 of 400 partitions are read: 800 × 10 / 400 = 20 GB of table. Three of 50 columns: 20 × 3 / 50 = 1.2 GB = 0.0012 TB, costing 0.006 dollars. That is 48 / 1.2 = 40 times less than the unpartitioned scan.</solution>
<check lang="python">
from datetime import date
days = (date(2025, 6, 11) - date(2025, 6, 1)).days
gb = 800 * days / 400 * 3 / 50
assert days == 10 and abs(gb - 1.2) < 1e-12
assert abs(gb / 1000 * 5 - 0.006) < 1e-12 and abs(48 / gb - 40) < 1e-9
</check>

<ask type="problem">The GB scanned, the cost in dollars, and the factor by which this scan is smaller than the one in Problem 1.</ask>
@@ verify
per_col = 800 / 50
assert per_col == 16 and 3 * per_col == 48 and abs(48 / 1000 * 5 - 0.24) < 1e-12
assert '48 GB' in learner and '0.24' in learner
from datetime import date
days = (date(2025, 6, 11) - date(2025, 6, 1)).days
assert days == 10
gb = 800 * days / 400 * 3 / 50
assert abs(gb - 1.2) < 1e-12 and '1.2 GB' in solution(reply)
assert abs(gb / 1000 * 5 - 0.006) < 1e-12 and '0.006' in solution(reply)
assert abs(48 / gb - 40) < 1e-9 and '40 times' in solution(reply)
assert '1.2 GB' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ correct-recall-consumer-groups
situation: after-correct
topic: Kafka consumer groups and rebalancing
voice: terse
level: medium
before: {"recall":[{"prompt":"Two consumers are in the same group on one topic. In normal operation, can both be assigned the same partition?","topic":"Kafka consumer groups and rebalancing","reviews":1,"lapses":0}]}
expect: {"verdict":"correct","heading":"Why this matters","must":["rebalanc"],"askMustNot":["last committed","again","reprocess","duplicat","twice"]}
noverify: a recall answer marked and an opening paragraph; nothing is computed
@@ tutor
<ask type="short">Two consumers are in the same group on one topic. In normal operation, can both be assigned the same partition?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
no. within a group each partition is owned by one consumer at a time, they split them. a 2nd group would get every record too
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"each partition is owned by one consumer at a time" is the rule, and you are right that a second group reads every record independently.</grade>

**Why this matters**

A consumer group is how Kafka spreads a topic's partitions over a set of worker processes, and moves them when a worker joins, leaves or stalls. That reassignment, the rebalance, is where lag spikes and repeated processing come from in production, so reading it correctly is most of operating a stream job. Kafka Streams applications and Kafka Connect sink connectors are consumer groups underneath, and scaling any consumer service comes down to how partitions are shared out.

<ask type="predict">A topic has 6 partitions and its group has 3 consumers, each owning 2. One consumer crashes after processing 40 records it had not yet committed offsets for. Which consumers read its 2 partitions next, and at which record do they start?</ask>
